rather than PDF statements. This would permit the use of most exists R
graphics. (I was going that direction with rpy2 by allowing
python-callbacks based devices, allowing agile prototyping of R devices).

Higher-level frameworks are already around (in R, grid being probably
the most heavily used, itself used to build higher-level systems such as
ggplot2), outside of R that's protovis, processing, etc... and that
would mean that we create a meta-framework that encompasses them all ?

Interactive graphics is a weak point in R, and this keeps surfacing
(sometimes around the time of GSoC:
https://stat.ethz.ch/pipermail/r-devel/2009-February/052214.html  ;-) ).
Getting a consensual (or unarguably best) framework for all to adopt
would be good... but may be a little hard for a summer project.

Laurent

