> This might be more a discussion to take on the R-dev list, but wouldn't that
> be the interface for graphical devices ? Then it would be a matter of
> creating say a graphical device that returns protovis statements rather than
> PDF statements. This would permit the use of most exists R graphics. (I was
> going that direction with rpy2 by allowing python-callbacks based devices,
> allowing agile prototyping of R devices).

No - a graphics framework designed twenty years ago for a completely
different computing environment and completely purpose is unlikely to
be the correct model for producing interactive graphics for the web.
Similarly, I don't see any point in trying to generate a meta-protocol
for the small (but growing) number of existing attempts, until someone
puts some serious effort into thinking about what such a framework
should look like.  Unfortunately current efforts seem to be focussing
on reproducing existing graphics, rather than developing a strong
underlying theory.

Hadley

